\title{The reuse cases of Reussir are too rare in a hash-consed store}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{store}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/X1.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: \src{docs/research/profiles/X1.md}, verdict 1 and section 4; the earlier analysis is part A of \src{docs/research/11-memory-reuse-parallelism.md}; [the profiling session](ses-JRSD). The idea examined was the few-bit reference count of Reussir, in the limit one bit that says unique or shared, which licenses in-place reuse. The verdicts rest on a census of the machine on the JVM with one worker; none of the four schemes was built.}
\ul{
\li{The cases the bit specialises are rare: extending the spine just built, extending or pruning the frame just built, bumping a Nat offset cell are 1.9 to 7.2 percent of the records, 0.4 to 3.4 percent of the new frames and a few hundred cells per corpus.}
\li{Working environments are already a stack-like slab: 1 copy in 162 M pushes over the nine census runs.}
\li{Where the case does occur, the source is held by a memo word or an environment; it was held by nothing in 0.0 percent of the cases in the plain machine.}
\li{Rewriting a hash-consed record in place trades an insert for a delete: it saves bytes, not a probe.}
}
\table{
\tr{\th{Scheme}\th{Verdict}}
\tr{\td{one bit "fresh and unpublished" on the newest record, rewrite before interning}\td{no-go}}
\tr{\td{an ephemeral region with mark and release per judgment, interning only on escape}\td{no-go as a speed-up}}
\tr{\td{saturating 2-bit counts in the record header}\td{no-go}}
\tr{\td{builders that assemble in registers and intern once}\td{already the design}}
}
\p{What the census points to instead is one bit per memo key; see [tabling records that are never found again](ins-RTT3). A later design applies the same thought to applications, see [identity on demand](ins-PMD4).}
\p{See also: [the decision against in-place reuse](dec-80ZZ); [the value model](design-value-model).}
